{"type":"opinions","digest":"evening","entries":[{"title":"AI experts in early 2026 express divided opinions on AIs impact, particularly on jobs and education,description:Predictions of widespread automation dominate recent discussions. Microsofts AI chief stated in February 2026 that all white-collar work could be automated within 18 months. In contrast, AI entrepreneur Vincent Luciani argues these forecasts overlook AIs potential to create new sectors representing the vast majority of tomorrow’s jobs.,url:https://www.insidehighered.com/news/tech-innovation/artificial-intelligence/2026/04/08/which-jobs-are-most-risk-age-ai},{title:AI isn’t killing jobs: Why AI is the Engine of Tomorrows Jobs","description":"Vincent Luciani argues that macro-analyses ignore implementation complexities and market rebuilding needs. Current layoffs result from market pressures, not AI replacement.","url":"https://www.artefact.com/news/ai-isnt-killing-jobs-why-ai-is-the-engine-of-tomorrows/"},{"title":"How AI is Showing Up in Our Classrooms","description":"Experts in higher education view AI as a tool for enhancement in educational settings, fostering student understanding.","url":"https://today.ucsd.edu/story/how-ai-is-showing-up-in-our-classrooms"}]}
